{
  "global_card_id": "momentum_candle",
  "global_canonical": "Momentum Candle",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Price Action",
  "aliases": [
    "Bullish Momentum Candle",
    "Bullish Momentum Pattern",
    "Momentum Pattern",
    "large candle",
    "strong momentum candle",
    "Momentum Candlestick",
    "Momentum Candlestick Pattern",
    "momentum play"
  ],
  "definition": "A candlestick signal of strong directional momentum entering the market, validating trend continuation or reversal near key levels. Identified by an unusually large real body relative to surrounding candles, with small or no wicks (especially on the momentum side) showing sustained pressure to the close. A green/bullish candle reflects strong buying; a red/bearish candle reflects strong selling. Most actionable at key levels or pattern completion points, and best used with additional confirmation rather than alone.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "A candlestick whose real body is at least twice the size of the preceding candles (typically the prior 3–5), ideally with small or no wicks, signaling strong directional momentum. A green candle indicates strong buying pressure; a red candle strong selling pressure. It often appears near key levels, validating trend continuation or reversal.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLb0LJvd-CX1wZ": "A two-candle pattern where the second candle's body is larger than the first, indicating strong directional momentum and signaling continuation. Both candles should be the same color. Most effective when the larger candle has no wick on the momentum side (e.g., no upper wick for bullish), showing sustained pressure to the close. Can also serve as an aggressive entry at key reversal points such as the second touch of a double top/bottom."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Compare the candle's real body to surrounding candles: SMC-Strong-Weak requires the body to be at least twice (ideally 3–5x) the largest body among the prior 3–5 candles, while SMC-Supplement frames it as a two-candle pattern where the second candle's body is visibly larger than the immediately preceding first candle.",
    "Both candles in the pattern should be the same color (SMC-Supplement); a green/bullish candle signals strong buying pressure, a red/bearish candle signals strong selling pressure.",
    "Only the real body size matters for classification; wicks are ignored for sizing, though small or no wicks are typical of strong momentum.",
    "SMC-Supplement emphasizes the larger candle should have no wick on the side aligned with the momentum direction (e.g., no upper wick for bullish, no lower wick for bearish), indicating sustained pressure until the close.",
    "The candle's direction should align with the expected trade and prevailing market trend for higher reliability (SMC-Supplement).",
    "It typically appears after consolidation or near key support/resistance, pattern completion points (second touch of a double top/bottom), trendlines, or areas of high convergence — and not within an existing strong trend where candles are already large.",
    "It often appears alongside or immediately after an engulfing candlestick at an area of high convergence (SMC-Supplement)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Treating any large candle as a momentum candle without verifying its real body size — at least twice the prior 3–5 candles (SMC-Strong-Weak) or larger than the immediately preceding candle (SMC-Supplement).",
    "Comparing only one or two previous candles instead of a sufficient sample of 3–5 to assess the size ratio (SMC-Strong-Weak).",
    "Ignoring market context: accepting any large candle without checking whether it follows consolidation, sits at a key level/convergence area, or instead falls within an already strong trend of similar large candles.",
    "Mistaking slow, grinding price action near a level as momentum.",
    "Misclassifying reversal patterns (e.g., hammers, shooting stars) as momentum candles due to small real bodies.",
    "Ignoring the wick condition — a momentum candle with an opposing wick indicates weaker conviction than one with no opposing wick (SMC-Supplement).",
    "Trading the pattern when it does not align with the overall market trend, or without considering conflict between primary and secondary trend directions (SMC-Supplement).",
    "Using the momentum candle as a standalone signal without additional confirmation such as volume, market structure, breakout validation, or confluence at a key area; a reaction to an area does not mean a reversal."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Momentum candles signal strong directional pressure, confirming support/resistance, breakouts, or continuation. Both sources treat them primarily as confirmation tools best combined with confluences (volume, market structure, key levels, trendlines, shrinking candles, double top/bottom). SMC-Strong-Weak frames concrete trade management: enter on the candle close, place a stop-loss below the recent swing low (bullish) or above the recent high (bearish), and target by risk/reward, with the candle's range potentially acting as support/resistance; a lack of momentum candles suggests a weak level. SMC-Supplement emphasizes its use at reversal points — e.g., the second touch of a double top within high convergence — where it can provide an aggressive early entry with better risk-to-reward before full pattern completion, though the reversal remains unconfirmed.",
  "merge_notes": "Key definitional difference: SMC-Strong-Weak defines the momentum candle as a single candle sized against the prior 3–5 candles (body ≥2x, ideally 3–5x), whereas SMC-Supplement defines it as a two-candle pattern (same color, second body larger than first) with a specific no-wick-on-momentum-side condition. SMC-Supplement adds the aggressive reversal entry use case (double top/bottom second touch, engulfing/convergence context) not present in SMC-Strong-Weak, which instead provides explicit stop-loss/target mechanics.",
